  "basics": true,
  "order": 3,
  "title": "Cognito: user pools and identity pools",
  "summary": "Cognito handles your application's own users, such as customers, not your workforce. A user pool is the user directory and sign-in service that issues tokens. An identity pool exchanges those tokens for temporary AWS credentials, through IAM roles.",
  "notes": [
    "User pool: sign-up, sign-in, MFA and federation with social or SAML/OIDC providers. It issues OIDC tokens (ID and access tokens) to your app.",
    "Many apps only need a user pool: API Gateway or your API validates the access token, and AWS credentials never reach the browser.",
    "Identity pool: when the app itself must call AWS (e.g. upload to S3), it trades the token for short-lived credentials of an IAM role. An optional guest role covers unauthenticated users.",
    "Scope the authenticated role per user with policy variables, e.g. limiting S3 access to a prefix named after ${cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub}.",
    "For workforce access to AWS accounts, use IAM Identity Center instead."
  ],
  "docs": [
